---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import { guides } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'track-where-enquiries-come-from')!;
const dateLabel = (d: string) => new Date(d).toLocaleDateString('en-IN', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' });

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    '@id': `${site.url}/guides/${guide.slug}#article`,
    headline: guide.title,
    description: guide.summary,
    image: `${site.url}/og-image.png`,
    datePublished: guide.published,
    dateModified: guide.updated,
    inLanguage: 'en-IN',
    mainEntityOfPage: `${site.url}/guides/${guide.slug}`,
    author: { '@id': `${site.url}/about#founder`, '@type': 'Person', name: site.founder, url: `${site.url}/about` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${site.url}/#org` },
    about: ['Marketing attribution', 'Google Analytics 4', 'UTM parameters', 'WhatsApp Business', 'Lead tracking'],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={guide.title}
  fullTitle="How to track where your enquiries come from | Nextmarg"
  description="A simple way for Indian small businesses to see which channel brings calls, WhatsApp chats and form enquiries: UTM links, tagged WhatsApp messages, GA4 events and a one-page sheet."
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', path: '/guides' }]}
  crumbName="Track where enquiries come from"
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:860px">
      <span class="kicker">Guide · Tracking</span>
      <h1 class="display">{guide.title}</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        By <a href="/about">{site.founder}</a>, founder of Nextmarg · Published {dateLabel(guide.published)}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <article class="wrap prose">
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:none">
        <strong>The short answer:</strong> give every channel its own tagged link, give every WhatsApp button its own pre-filled message, count
        calls, chats and forms as events in Google Analytics 4, ask every new customer how they found you, and write the totals in one sheet
        each month. After three months you will know which rupee brings which enquiry, and you can stop paying for what doesn't.
      </p>

      <h2>Why likes and visits are not enough</h2>
      <p>
        Followers, reach and website visits are easy to count, but they don't pay salaries. What matters is how many people called, messaged
        or filled a form, where they came from, and how many became customers. Most small businesses in India get enquiries on three
        channels: phone, WhatsApp and website forms. Track all three.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 1: Tag every link you share</h2>
      <p>
        Add UTM tags to the website link on your Google Business Profile, Instagram bio, Facebook page, WhatsApp status, emails and ads. A
        UTM tag is a short code at the end of a link, for example
        <code>?utm_source=instagram&amp;utm_medium=social&amp;utm_campaign=bio</code>. Google Analytics then shows those visits separately.
        Google's free Campaign URL Builder makes the links for you. Keep a list of every tagged link so the names stay consistent.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2: Give each WhatsApp button its own message</h2>
      <p>
        A <code>wa.me</code> link can carry a pre-filled message. Use a different one per place: "Hi, I saw your Instagram post", "Hi, I found
        you on Google", "Hi, I'm on your pricing page". When the chat arrives, you know where it came from without asking. In WhatsApp
        Business, add a label to each chat, such as "Instagram" or "Google", so you can count them at month end.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3: Count calls, chats and forms in Google Analytics 4</h2>
      <p>
        Set up Google Tag Manager to send an event to GA4 whenever someone taps a phone number, a WhatsApp button or an email link, and when a
        form is actually submitted, not just opened. Mark the form submission as a key event. Your GA4 reports then show enquiries by
        source, not just visits. Ask for consent before analytics cookies load, as India's DPDP Act expects.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 4: Ask every new customer one question</h2>
      <p>
        Calls from Google Maps or a printed flyer don't always pass through your website. So ask every new customer "How did you hear about
        us?" and note it in your billing software, CRM or a simple sheet. If you run ads, Google and Meta can also show you calls made
        straight from an ad.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 5: One sheet, once a month</h2>
      <p>On the first working day of each month, fill in one row per channel:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Channel:</strong> Google Maps, Google search, Instagram, ads, referrals, walk-in.</li>
        <li><strong>Enquiries:</strong> calls, WhatsApp chats and forms, from GA4, WhatsApp labels and your notes.</li>
        <li><strong>Customers:</strong> how many of those enquiries paid.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost:</strong> ad spend, agency fees and your time.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost per customer:</strong> cost divided by customers.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Look at the trend over three months, not one. Then move money and time towards the channel with the lowest cost per customer, and
        fix or drop the rest.
      </p>

      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Counting form opens or page views as leads. Count only real submissions, calls and chats.</li>
        <li>Using one WhatsApp link everywhere, so every chat looks the same.</li>
        <li>Forgetting offline sources such as referrals and walk-ins, which are often the best ones.</li>
        <li>Judging a channel after two weeks. Most need a few months to show a clear pattern.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Want it set up for you?</h2>
      <p>
        Every Nextmarg plan includes this tracking. Our Tracking Health Check audits and fixes GA4 and Tag Manager for an existing site and
        gives you one dashboard. See <a href="/services#prices">prices</a>, or start with the free
        <a href="/digital-health-score">Digital Health Score</a>, which checks your tracking along with Google and AI search.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">Last updated {dateLabel(guide.updated)}.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <CtaBand title="Do you know your cost per enquiry?" />
</Base>
